    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:authorConclusion",
      "object": "We observed a negative influence of the locus of control attributed to the coach on the self regulation of eating attitudes in contexts of negative affect, social interaction and lack of anticipation of consequences on performance. The coach and sports firends contribute to maladaptive patterns of accomplishment and function as negative predictors of the self regulation of eating attitudes in several contexts. The results showed the positive influence of an internal locus of control on the self regulation of eating attitudes in the context of social interaction. We observed a negative influence of self regulatory eating attitudes on disordered eating in contexts of negative affect, social interaction and the absence of anticipation of consequences on performance. This study demonstrated the influence of an internal locus of control on disordered eating as mediated by self regulatory eating attitudes in a context of social interaction and the influence of an external locus of control, attributed to the coach and sports friends on disordered eating as mediated by self regulatory eating attitudes in context of negative affect, social interaction and an absence of anticipation of consequences on performance. This study did not reveal a significant influence of a locus of control attributed to the family or luck on the self regulation of eating attitudes in contexts of temptation or negative affect. The self regulation subscales were not significant in the contexts of food temptation and a lack of compensatory strategies."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:limites",
      "object": "The data were self reported, which suggests that social desirability may have been a bias. The study used correlational and cross sectional methods, which limits the generalization of the relationships."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:perspectives",
      "object": "The social environment has a powerful impact on the development of disordered eating in athletes; the influence of these social relationship on athletes self regulatory eating attitudes merits further study, through an emphasis on locus of control and/or relationship quality. A longitudinal study would determine the stability of this impact and an experimental study would help to etablish the direction of the causality. \nThe use of locus of control and self regulatory eating attitudes as concurrent variables may be useful to sports psychology practitioners in their work with athletes. They might also be used to evaluate an athlete ability to engage in new and better adapted eating strategies and to determine whether the development of new eating attitudes would benefit performance outcomes."
    },
